---
// Kills the first-paint WHITE canvas on a cold load, and tints the browser's own
// chrome to match the app.
//
// The browser shows a blank canvas while the render-blocking stylesheets download, and a
// blank canvas defaults to WHITE unless the page declares `color-scheme`. Since #962 the
// only `color-scheme` lives inside the per-palette token CSS (`html[data-palette][data-mode]`),
// which is exactly that late-loading render-blocking CSS — so during the cold-load window the
// browser has no dark signal and paints white. (The on-demand engine load widened that window,
// which is why it became visible.)
//
// The load-bearing fix is the STATIC `<meta>` below: it's in the server-rendered HTML, so the
// browser honors it for the blank canvas IMMEDIATELY (no JS, no CSS wait) — Safari included.
// `content="light dark"` tracks the OS, so a dark-OS visitor gets a dark canvas from frame one.
// The inline script then refines it to the *app* mode (localStorage) so a dark-app visitor on a
// light-OS device is corrected too, as early as script can run.
//
// ── theme-color: the browser toolbar, tinted to the app ────────────────────────────
// iOS Safari paints its URL bar and bottom bar with `<meta name="theme-color">`. Without
// one it picks its own gray, which is why the Studio read as a page sitting inside a
// browser rather than as an app: the chrome above the deck was a different color from
// everything below it. A web page cannot HIDE Safari's toolbars — only "Add to Home
// Screen" does that, and the manifest already declares `display: standalone` for it —
// but it can make them disappear into the app, which is most of the win.
//
// The value has to be the RESOLVED `--bg`, not a `prefers-color-scheme` pair: the app's
// mode is its own (localStorage, not the OS), and `--bg` differs per palette — onyx-dark
// is #000000 where indaco-dark is not. A hardcoded pair would be wrong for eleven of the
// twelve palettes. So: a static value for the cold-load window, then read the computed
// token once CSS has landed, and re-read whenever `data-palette` / `data-mode` change.
---
<meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
<meta name="theme-color" content="#FFFFFF" />
<script is:inline>
	(function () {
		try {
			var m = localStorage.getItem('lattice-docs-mode');
			var dark = m === 'dark' || (m !== 'light' && window.matchMedia && matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches);
			var scheme = dark ? 'dark' : 'light';
			document.documentElement.style.colorScheme = scheme;
			var mt = document.querySelector('meta[name="color-scheme"]');
			if (mt) mt.setAttribute('content', scheme);

			var tc = document.querySelector('meta[name="theme-color"]');
			if (!tc) return;
			// Cold-load guess, before any palette CSS exists: mode-appropriate, so the
			// toolbar never flashes white on a dark app.
			tc.setAttribute('content', dark ? '#0B0F14' : '#FFFFFF');

			// Then the truth: whatever `--bg` actually resolves to for this palette+mode.
			var sync = function () {
				try {
					var bg = getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--bg').trim();
					// `--bg` is `light-dark(...)` in source but computes to a single color;
					// only accept something that reads as one, so a half-loaded stylesheet
					// cannot write garbage into the toolbar.
					if (/^(#|rgb|oklch|oklab|color\()/i.test(bg)) tc.setAttribute('content', bg);
				} catch (e) {}
			};
			if (document.readyState === 'loading') addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', sync);
			else sync();
			addEventListener('load', sync);
			// The palette and mode are attributes on <html>; the site chrome and the Studio
			// both drive them, so observing the element covers every path without either
			// needing to know this exists.
			new MutationObserver(sync).observe(document.documentElement, {
				attributes: true,
				attributeFilter: ['data-palette', 'data-mode', 'style'],
			});
		} catch (e) {}
	})();
</script>
